==English==
{{was wotd|2011|December|12}}

===Etymology===
{{calque|en|la|advocātus diabolī||the devil’s advocate}}, a popular title given to the officer dealing with the [[canonization]] process in the 16th c.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-devil's advocate.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[devil]]'s [[advocate]]}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} One who [[debate|debates]] from a [[viewpoint]] which he or she may not actually [[hold]], usually to [[determine]] its [[validity]] or simply for the sake of [[argument]].
#: {{ux|en|I don't really believe all that – I was just playing '''devil's advocate'''.}}
#* {{quote-av
|en
|medium=motion picture
|title=Lost in America
|year=1985
|role=David Howard
|actor=Albert Brooks
|writer=Albert Brooks
|passage=Now, play '''devil's advocate'''. Can't you live 20 years on $145,000 if you're living out of a motor home and just eating and painting and writing books? I mean, this is what we talked about when we were 19. Remember, we kept saying, "Let's find ourselves," but we didn't have a dollar! So, we watched television instead.}}
# {{lb|en|historical|Roman Catholic}} A [[canon]] [[lawyer]] appointed by the [[Church]] to [[argue]] against the [[canonization]] of the proposed [[candidate]].

